        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="IICn_15-2.lnote.oe.dagger-2" n="†" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">321</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de"><hi rend="italic">vgl.</hi> 15, 1</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">cf. 15, 1</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="IICn_15-2.lnote.oe.3-5" n="3" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">321</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">homines <hi rend="italic">DiCbS</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">homines DiCbS</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="IICn_15-2.lnote.de.1" n="1" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">204</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">2</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">Benutzt durch Hn 34, 3, übersetzt durch Leis Wl 15, 2</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">Used by Hn 34, 3, translated by Leis Wl 15, 2</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="IICn_15-2.lnote.de.2" n="2" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">204</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">2</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">Der Ggs. zu <hi rend="italic">unlagu</hi> und <hi rend="italic">undom</hi> in 15, 1³</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">The contrast to <hi rend="italic">unlagu</hi> and <hi rend="italic">undom</hi> in 15, 1³</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="IICn_15-2.lnote.de.3" n="3" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">204</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">2</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de"><hi rend="italic">rihtdom</hi> (zu verbinden als ein Wort), wie B liest, scheint unbedenklich laut der vielen Komposita <hi rend="italic">riht</hi>- (<hi rend="italic">s. Wb</hi>); es fehlt aber Toller</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude"><hi rend="italic">rihtdom</hi> (to be joined as one word), as B reads, seems unobjectionable in view of the many compounds in <hi rend="italic">riht</hi>- (<hi rend="italic">see Wb</hi>); but it is missing from Toller</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="IICn_15-2.lnote.de.4" n="4" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">204</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">3</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">Ich sehe hierin Rechtsweigerung (<hi rend="italic">s. d. Gl</hi> 6), laut der 3 Übersetzer und beider Benutzer; so Cohn <hi rend="italic">Justizweigerung</hi> 112 und Schmid im Glossar S. 638 gegen andere frühere Meinung (Justizweigerung).—Andere finden hierin Urteilschelte (<hi rend="italic">s. d. Gl</hi> 3). Aber müsste dann nicht hinzugefügt sein: ‘es sei denn, er dringt mit der Schelte durch’? Unmöglich wäre ferner, dass jemand das Urteil dreier Instanzen schalt, während ganz denkbar ist, dass er es trotz der drei nicht befolgte</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">I see in this a refusal of justice (<hi rend="italic">see</hi> that word in <hi rend="italic">Gl</hi> 6), following the 3 translators and both borrowers; so Cohn <hi rend="italic">Justizweigerung</hi> 112 and Schmid in his Glossary p. 638, against another, earlier opinion (Justizweigerung).—Others find in this the challenging of a judgement (<hi rend="italic">see</hi> that word in <hi rend="italic">Gl</hi> 3). But in that case would it not have had to be added: ‘unless he succeeds with the challenge’? Furthermore, it would be impossible for someone to challenge the judgement of three instances, whereas it is quite conceivable that he failed to comply with it despite the three</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="IICn_15-2.lnote.de.5" n="5" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">204</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">3</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">Schmid verbindet <hi rend="italic">on Engla lage</hi> mit <hi rend="italic">geweorðe</hi>, übersehend, dass <hi rend="italic">swa</hi> davor sich bezieht auf voriges <hi rend="italic">swa</hi>. Cons und Thorpe verstehen richtig</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">Schmid connects <hi rend="italic">on Engla lage</hi> with <hi rend="italic">geweorðe</hi>, overlooking that the <hi rend="italic">swa</hi> before it refers back to the preceding <hi rend="italic">swa</hi>. Cons and Thorpe understand it correctly</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="IICn_15-2.lnote.de.6" n="6" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">204</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">3</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">Falls <hi rend="italic">præsidet</hi> in Cons richtig, ist der Gerichtsherr als ‘Präsident’ bezeichnet</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">If <hi rend="italic">præsidet</hi> in Cons is correct, the lord of jurisdiction is described as ‘president’</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="IICn_15-2.lnote.de.7" n="7" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">204</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">3</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">‘Ungehorsam’ (<hi rend="italic">s. d. Gl</hi> 4. b); Hn rechnet zu Normann. Schilling richtig um; dagegen über Inst <hi rend="italic">s. o</hi>. 15, 1⁶</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">‘Disobedience’ (<hi rend="italic">see</hi> that word in <hi rend="italic">Gl</hi> 4. b); Hn correctly converts into Norman shillings; on Inst, by contrast, <hi rend="italic">see above</hi> 15, 1⁶</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="IICn_15-2.lnote.de.8" n="8" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">204</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">3</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">‘40’ auch Hn 34, 3. Leis Wl 42; dagegen Hn 35, 1. 87, 5; <hi rend="italic">vgl. Gl</hi> Ungehorsam 25e</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">‘40’ also Hn 34, 3. Leis Wl 42; but contrast Hn 35, 1. 87, 5; <hi rend="italic">cf. Gl</hi> Ungehorsam 25e</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="IICn_15-2.lnote.de.9" n="9" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">204</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">3</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de"><hi rend="italic">Ebd</hi>. 25a; Hundred 18; B's <hi rend="italic">XX</hi> ist Schreibfehler</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude"><hi rend="italic">Ibid</hi>. 25a; Hundred 18; B's <hi rend="italic">XX</hi> is a scribal error</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="IICn_15-2.lnote.de.10" n="10" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">204</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">3</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">Das Immunitätsgericht bleibt unerwähnt. Sofern es aus dem Volksgericht entsprungen war, deckte es sich mit dem Hundred; <hi rend="italic">s. d. Gl</hi> 23. Die andere private Gerichtsbarkeit, allein senioralen oder manorialen Ursprungs, bleibt ausserhalb des Bereichs staatlicher Gesetzgebung</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">The court of an immunity is not mentioned. In so far as it had sprung from the popular court, it coincided with the hundred; <hi rend="italic">see</hi> that word in <hi rend="italic">Gl</hi> 23. The other private jurisdiction, of purely seigniorial or manorial origin, remains outside the sphere of state legislation</seg></note>
        <note type="editorNote" subtype="endnote" xml:id="IICn_15-2.robnote.n.1" n="1" source="#robertson"><bibl><biblScope unit="page">354</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="en">Cf. Leis Wl. 42 f.; Hn. 34, 3.</seg></note>
        <note type="editorNote" subtype="endnote" xml:id="IICn_15-2.robnote.n.2" n="2" source="#robertson"><bibl><biblScope unit="page">354</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="en">The regular fine payable to the hundred, cf. III Edm. 2.</seg></note>
        <note type="editorNote" subtype="footnote" xml:id="IICn_15-2.robnote.fn.2" n="2" source="#robertson"><bibl><biblScope unit="page">182</biblScope></bibl><label>dóm</label><seg type="original" xml:lang="en"><hi rend="italic">rihtne dom</hi> G, A, Ld; Liebermann suggests the reading <hi rend="italic">rihtdom</hi> in B.</seg></note>
        <note type="editorNote" subtype="footnote" xml:id="IICn_15-2.robnote.fn.3" n="3" source="#robertson"><bibl><biblScope unit="page">182</biblScope></bibl><label>xxx</label><seg type="original" xml:lang="en">G, A, Ld; xx B.</seg></note>
